        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 10: Disaster Recovery, Diagnostics, and Troubleshooting\nP\u00e1ginas: 404-435\n\u2022 Technical requirements  (404)\n\u2022 Planning for disaster recovery  (405)\n  \u2022 A brief introduction to risk management  (405)\n  \u2022 Risk calculation  (407)\n  \u2022 Designing a DRP  (408)\n\u2022 Backing up and restoring the system  (409)\n  \u2022 Disk cloning solutions  (410)\n\u2022 Introducing common Linux diagnostic tools for troubleshooting  (416)\n  \u2022 Tools for troubleshooting boot issues  (416)\n  \u2022 Tools for troubleshooting general system issues  (418)\n  \u2022 Tools for troubleshooting network issues  (427)\n  \u2022 Tools for troubleshooting hardware issues  (433)\n\u2022 Summary  (434)\n\u2022 Questions  (435)\n\u2022 Further reading  (435)\n10\nDisaster Recovery, Diagnostics,\nand Troubleshooting\nIn this chapter, you will learn how to do a system backup and restore in a disaster recovery scenario,\nand how to diagnose and troubleshoot a common array of problems. These are skills that each Linux\nsystem administrator needs to have if they wish to be prepared for worst-case scenarios such as power\noutages, theft, or hardware failure. The world\u2019s IT backbone runs on Linux and we need to be prepared\nfor anything that life throws at us.\nIn this chapter, we\u2019re going to cover the following main topics:\n\u2022I Planning for disaster recovery\n\u2022I Backing up and restoring the system\n\u2022I Introducing common Linux diagnostic tools for troubleshooting",
